! Tasks

Due dates for your tiddlers, and a list per tag that shows what is coming
up and what has already slipped past.

!! The idea
A task is an ordinary tiddler with a ''due date''. Tag your tasks with
whatever groups them — a subject, a project, a client, a room of the house —
and each of those tags gets a list, in date order, with anything overdue in
red. The plugin brings no tags of its own: a list exists for any tag you
have used, and stops existing when you stop using it.

Nothing is kept in a hidden store: the due date is a `due` field on the
tiddler itself, so your tasks are your tiddlers and they travel with the
wiki rather than with this plugin.

!! Giving a tiddler a due date
Open it and press ''◴'' in the tiddler toolbar. Pick a date, and a time if
the hour matters — a date on its own falls due at the end of that day, so
something due "on the 25th" stays on time until the 25th is over. ''Today'',
''Tomorrow'' and ''Next week'' are there for the common cases, and
''Unschedule'' takes the date off again.

Or type the date in words, in the box under the picker: `fri`, `tomorrow`,
`+3d`, `in 2 weeks`, `25 nov`, `25/11`, `next monday`. Add a time on the end
if you want one — `fri 9am`, `tomorrow 14:30`. A day or month that has
already gone by is read as next year's.

You can also type a date straight into the `due` field. It is stored the way
TiddlyWiki stores `created` and `modified` — `20261125235959999`, a UTC
stamp — but `2026-11-25` and `2026-11-25T09:30` are understood too and get
tidied into that form the next time the task is edited through the plugin.

!! The task list
Open the ''Tasks'' tiddler for every task in the wiki, and press a tag along
the top to narrow it to that tag. Tasks are ordered by due date throughout,
grouped under ''Overdue'', ''Today'', ''Tomorrow'', ''This week'', ''Later''
and ''No due date''.

* ''Overdue'' tasks are red, and the heading tells you how many there are.
* The tick box on the left marks a task done.
* ''+1d'' pushes a task back a day. On a task that is already overdue it
means tomorrow, not another day in the past.
* ''◴'' on a row changes the date.
* ''+'' adds a task. Give it a title, a tag and a date; a title that is
already in the wiki keeps whatever it says and simply gains the tag and the
date.

Tagged tiddlers with no due date gather under ''No due date'' — somewhere
for the things you have noted down but not yet scheduled.

!! Repeats
Under ''Repeats'' in the same popup: ''Daily'', ''Weekly'',
''Fortnightly'', ''Monthly'', ''Yearly'', or type your own — `every 3 days`,
`every 2 weeks`.

Ticking off a recurring task moves it to its next turn instead of finishing
it, so it never leaves the list. One left alone for a month skips to the
next date still to come rather than to another one already past, and a
monthly task due on the 31st falls on the end of February rather than
sliding into March. There is no undo: if you tick one by mistake, set the
date again in the popup.

The day of the week is not part of the grammar because it does not need to
be — a weekly task due on a Monday already comes round on Mondays.

!! Priority
''High'', ''Medium'' or ''Low'', shown as a dot before the title. Two tasks
due the same day are ordered by priority, so the urgent one is on top. It
only breaks ties: a low-priority task due today still comes before an
urgent one due next week.

!! Not before
A ''start'' date keeps a task out of the way until the day arrives. Until
then it sits under ''Not started yet'', folded away at the foot of the list,
and it is not counted as overdue however long its due date has been and
gone. Useful for the thing you cannot begin until something else happens.

!! Clearing a backlog
The ''Overdue'' heading has a ''push to today'' button, which moves
everything overdue to the end of today in one go.

!! Knowing without looking
The sidebar carries a count of everything overdue, across every tag, and
clicking it opens the task list. Turn it off under ''⚙''.

!! The list on a tag's own tiddler
Open the tiddler for one of your tags and the same list appears under its
text, so a tag tiddler doubles as the view for it. Turn that off under ''⚙''
on the Tasks tiddler.

!! Putting a list anywhere
`<<tasks YourTag>>` drops the list for a tag into any tiddler, so a daily
note or a project tiddler can carry its own.

!! Done
Finishing a task writes the time to a `done` field and moves it to the
''Done'' list at the foot of the view, hidden until you ask for it. Tick the
box again to put it back.

!! Fields
Everything lives in fields on the task itself:

* `due` — when it is due, as a 17 digit stamp. No field, or an empty one,
means unscheduled.
* `done` — when it was finished. Absent or empty means it is still open.
* `repeat` — how often it comes round: `daily`, `weekly`, `every 3 days`.
Anything unreadable is pointed out in the popup and treated as no repeat.
* `priority` — `high`, `medium` or `low`.
* `start` — the day it becomes live; until then it is put aside.
* `lastdone` — when a recurring task was last ticked off.

!! Settings
Under ''⚙'' on the Tasks tiddler:

* the time of day a bare date falls due — 23:59 unless you change it
* whether a tag's own tiddler carries its list
* whether the sidebar shows a count of overdue tasks
* the ''overdue colour''. Left blank, the plugin picks a red to suit your
system's light or dark setting. That is a guess: a TiddlyWiki palette is
chosen separately from the system setting, so a dark palette on a light
system gets a red too dark to read against it. Set any CSS colour here and
it is used instead.
